(world.nc -- the world, as an errand.)

(Everything else here is the world, or a picture of it, or a way of)
(working it out. This is neither. It is a list of places to go, written)
(for a machine that cuts, in the language such machines are sent out in.)
(The machine is not told what it is making. It is told where to be next,)
(and how fast, and how deep, and it goes. The world is what is found on)
(the bench when it has come back.)

(Wanted: one blank, eighty millimetres square and three thick, of brass)
(or boxwood or whatever is to hand. Soap will do, and forgives more.)
(A cutter with a point to it, sixty degrees or thereabouts.)
(Put zero at the middle of the blank and on its top face. Millimetres.)

(It cuts a fifth of a millimetre deep and no deeper: one circle, which)
(is the world, and two words inside it, which are what it says. There is)
(no land and no sea. At this depth they are the same.)

(Every other version here was made by putting something where there had)
(been nothing. This one is made by taking a little away, and the world)
(is the part that is missing. Keep the swarf if you like. It is the)
(only copy of what the world is not.)

(Read it through before you run it. One should, with worlds, and with)
(anything that moves a sharp point about on its own say-so.)
(Run it in the air first, with the blank left off. The machine will not)
(know the difference, and will be exactly as pleased with the result.)

(The terms. Millimetres, measured from the middle, on the flat.)
G21 G90 G17 G94
(Up, out of the way of everything, before anything turns.)
G0 Z5
M3 S12000
(Four seconds, for the spindle to make up its mind.)
G4 P4

(The world. One circle, thirty-six millimetres from the middle to the)
(edge, cut the way the sun goes. It is begun in the east and ends there.)
G0 X36 Y0
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G2 X36 Y0 I-36 J0 F180
G0 Z2


(h. A post, and a lean-to against it.)
G0 X-29.1 Y6
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G1 X-29.1 Y-4 F180
G0 Z2
G0 X-29.1 Y0
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G1 X-28.5 Y1.4 F180
G1 X-27.1 Y2
G1 X-25.7 Y1.4
G1 X-25.1 Y0
G1 X-25.1 Y-4
G0 Z2

(e. Across first, then round, and it stops short of closing. They do.)
G0 X-23.1 Y-1
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G1 X-17.1 Y-1 F180
G3 X-17.502 Y-2.5 I-3 J0
G0 Z2

(l. Down.)
G0 X-15.1 Y6
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G1 X-15.1 Y-4 F180
G0 Z2

(l. Down again. The machine does not find this dull.)
G0 X-13.1 Y6
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G1 X-13.1 Y-4 F180
G0 Z2

(o. A small world inside the large one. Nothing is meant by it.)
G0 X-5.1 Y-1
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G2 X-5.1 Y-1 I-3 J0 F180
G0 Z2

(The gap between the words. It is five millimetres of nothing, and it)
(is crossed in the air. The blank keeps it exactly as it was given.)

(w. Four strokes, and the only letter with no curve in it at all.)
G0 X-0.1 Y2
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G1 X1.4 Y-4 F180
G1 X2.9 Y1
G1 X4.4 Y-4
G1 X5.9 Y2
G0 Z2

(o. The same as the last one, five places along.)
G0 X13.9 Y-1
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G2 X13.9 Y-1 I-3 J0 F180
G0 Z2

(r. A post, and a start made on something.)
G0 X15.9 Y2
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G1 X15.9 Y-4 F180
G0 Z2
G0 X15.9 Y0
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G1 X16.7 Y1.5 F180
G1 X17.9 Y2
G1 X19.1 Y1.6
G0 Z2

(l. Down, for the third time.)
G0 X21.1 Y6
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G1 X21.1 Y-4 F180
G0 Z2

(d. Round, then the long stroke. That is the last cut there is.)
G0 X29.1 Y-1
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G2 X29.1 Y-1 I-3 J0 F180
G0 Z2
G0 X29.1 Y6
G1 Z-0.2 F60
G1 X29.1 Y-4 F180
G0 Z2

(No stop is cut after it. There has never been one, and a machine is)
(the last party that should be trusted to add punctuation.)

(Up, and stopped, and back to the middle, where it began.)
G0 Z5
M5
G0 X0 Y0
(That is the errand. Brush it off and hold it to the light.)
M30
